        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Glistening silver embroidery decorates the pocket flap of this blue silk waistcoat dating from the 1740s. Silver thread, purl (metal thread wound into a short coil), strip and spangles (sequins) comprise the decoration which covers the pocket flaps and the front and lower edges of the waistcoat. Each pocket flap has a button stitched on both corners and four sewn below. All are decorative and there are no buttonholes. \n\nThis waistcoat could have formed part of a wedding ensemble, as the combination of blue and silver was fashionable for the bridegroom in the 18th century. In Samuel Richardson’s novel, <i>Pamela</i> (1741), Mr B. wears such a waistcoat to his wedding to Pamela. Similarly, the hero of <i>The Adventures of Sir Lancelot Greaves</i>, by Tobias Smollet (1760) marries in a ‘white coat and blue sattin vest, both embroidered with silver’."@en ;
